# Response headers for static assets.
#
# This file exists because the Worker cannot do the job. [assets] + run_worker_first
# is deliberately scoped to the HTML entry points (see wrangler.toml), so a request
# for /assets/images/x.jpg is answered by the asset server and src/index.js never
# runs. A cache-control helper was written there first and silently did nothing:
# verified with curl, versioned and unversioned URLs both still came back
# max-age=0. _headers is applied by the asset server itself, with no Worker
# invocation and no edge-cache cost.

# ── product photos ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The default was `max-age=0, must-revalidate`, so every returning visitor
# re-checked all 22 photos on the first screen — 22 round trips to be told nothing
# changed, which on a slow connection is seconds of blank cards. Cloudflare's own
# image resizer was flagging it in a response header:
#   warning: cf-images 299 "cache-control is too restrictive"
#
# WHY NOT `immutable`, WHICH IS THE OBVIOUS ANSWER
#
# Filenames here are not stable. All eight poster photos were re-cropped in place
# under their existing names, so a path is NOT a durable identity for its contents.
# `immutable` tells a browser never to revalidate, which would have pinned the
# uncropped versions for everyone who had already visited, with no way to clear it.
#
# _headers matches on path only — it cannot see the ?v=<hash> that product URLs now
# carry — so it cannot grant `immutable` just to the URLs that have earned it. The
# TTL therefore has to be one that is safe for the WORST case, a bare unversioned
# path, and a day is that: any replaced photo is picked up within one, and in
# practice much sooner, because every URL the site emits carries the content hash
# and a re-crop changes it. stale-while-revalidate then keeps the following month of
# visits instant, refreshing in the background rather than making anyone wait.
/assets/images/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400, stale-while-revalidate=2592000

# ── css and js ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Deliberately SHORTER than the photos, which is the opposite of the usual advice —
# and only because these filenames are not fingerprinted. A photo is content-hashed
# in its URL (?v=<hash>), so it can be cached hard and a change is a different URL.
# style.css and main.js are not: the same URL has to start meaning something new
# the moment a deploy lands, and the only lever for that is the TTL.
#
# THIS USED TO SAY max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=604800, described as "an
# hour with a week of background revalidation behind it". That misreads what SWR
# does. It does not revalidate quietly and serve fresh — it serves the STALE file
# to that visitor and revalidates afterwards, so a returning phone could render a
# week-old stylesheet. It did: a layout fix was verified live, correct in a fresh
# browser, and still visibly broken on a device that had the old CSS.
#
# A minute, and no stale window. The cost is one conditional request per asset per
# visit, answered with a 304 of a few hundred bytes. The cost of the old value was
# not knowing whether what you are looking at is what you deployed.
#
# The real fix is fingerprinting these filenames, at which point they can be
# immutable for a year. Until then the number is bounded by how long a bad deploy
# may persist, not by how big the files are.
/assets/css/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, must-revalidate

/assets/js/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, must-revalidate

# ── the image manifest ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Read by the Worker on catalogue requests and it is how a newly pushed photo
# becomes visible, so it must not be held. Short, and revalidated.
/assets/images.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60, must-revalidate
